Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 21:06:20 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] cpu: thread affinity and priority on Android (#185)

bionic defines __ANDROID__ but not __gnu_linux__, so the Linux branch that
already carried an Android sched_setaffinity call was never compiled there:
--cpu-mask, --cpu-strict and --prio were silently ignored. Unpinned, a
compute thread that wakes from sleep can be packed onto a core that already
runs one, and the spinning barrier then collapses a whole run (the 12R showed
three of five threads on the X3 and reps of 1.2-4 tok/s beside 12). Pinned
to the five big cores, tg128 at hot 64 holds 12.68 tok/s over six reps.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
index 43af94f..3b48c34 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
@@ -2708,8 +2708,10 @@ static bool ggml_thread_apply_priority(int32_t prio) {
     return true;
 }
 
-#elif defined(__gnu_linux__)
+#elif defined(__gnu_linux__) || defined(__ANDROID__)
 // TODO: this may not work on BSD, to be verified
+// #185: bionic does not define __gnu_linux__, so without __ANDROID__ here
+// --cpu-mask and --cpu-strict were silently ignored on Android.
 
 static bool ggml_thread_apply_affinity(const bool * mask) {
     cpu_set_t cpuset;
